materials, hospitals and equipment.

00:06:34.435 --> 00:06:38.106
Even if everything goes perfectly
with the engineering

00:06:38.106 --> 00:06:41.901
and the astronauts are perfectly trained
physically,

00:06:41.901 --> 00:06:45.696
the mission could still fail
due to the human factor

00:06:45.863 --> 00:06:50.535
isolation, being far away from home,
living in a confined, high stress

00:06:50.535 --> 00:06:54.956
environment, it could lead to depression
and behavioral changes.

00:06:54.956 --> 00:06:59.210
I mean, we really
saw this during COVID, right?

00:07:03.005 --> 00:07:04.507
There are several places on

00:07:04.507 --> 00:07:07.009
Earth that share similarities
with the red planet.

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:10.930
In fact, some of them are used
as training grounds for astronauts.

00:07:11.222 --> 00:07:14.851
But these aren't necessarily
the best representation

00:07:14.851 --> 00:07:17.937
of how people are actually
going to live there, because Mars doesn't

00:07:17.937 --> 00:07:22.358
really have a living surface with zero
air pressure.

00:07:22.483 --> 00:07:24.527
Mars is essentially a vacuum.

00:07:24.735 --> 00:07:26.863
You just can't survive without a suit.

00:07:26.863 --> 00:07:31.451
You would black out in like 20 seconds,
and if you tried breathing,

00:07:31.451 --> 00:07:34.787
your lungs would rupture
and you would die within minutes.

00:07:34.996 --> 00:07:38.374
In fact,
even the harshest environment on Earth

00:07:38.374 --> 00:07:40.960
is still going to feel like
heaven compared to Mars.

00:07:41.169 --> 00:07:44.547
But I think there's
a few manmade environments that do have

00:07:44.547 --> 00:07:46.757
some similarities to living on Mars.

00:07:56.976 --> 00:08:00.688
The structure of a lighthouse
is a series of interconnected components

00:08:00.688 --> 00:08:04.025
with a specific function
controlled by the inhabitants.

00:08:04.066 --> 00:08:05.693
Your everyday life and duties

00:08:05.693 --> 00:08:08.529
are entangled with the structure
as long as you live there.

00:08:08.696 --> 00:08:11.616
In some ways,
you become a part of the lighthouse,

00:08:11.782 --> 00:08:13.451
but you can still go out and take

00:08:13.451 --> 00:08:16.412
fresh air and experience
the environment outside the lighthouse.

00:08:16.787 --> 00:08:19.165
What happens
if you take the concept of a lighthouse

00:08:19.207 --> 00:08:20.791
to the extreme.

00:08:24.545 --> 00:08:26.088
just like on a mars habitat

00:08:26.088 --> 00:08:29.926
People in a submarine work together
towards completing a mission that could

00:08:29.926 --> 00:08:30.927
last months.

00:08:30.927 --> 00:08:35.431
Each person has a very specific job
and they have to work together to succeed.

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:38.559
And they do this while crammed inside

00:08:38.559 --> 00:08:42.813
a pressurized steel hull
with very little personal space.

00:08:43.064 --> 00:08:45.191
So the level of pressure 

00:08:45.191 --> 00:08:46.776
get it?

00:08:46.776 --> 00:08:49.904
 is both physical and psychological.

00:08:50.112 --> 00:08:54.075
In each of these self-contained
conditions, there's a real profound

00:08:54.075 --> 00:08:57.495
connection between the inhabitants
and the places they live

00:08:57.912 --> 00:09:01.541
because it can determine
whether you live or die.

00:09:06.504 --> 00:09:08.923
The most important
part of the Martian habitat

00:09:08.923 --> 00:09:13.094
is the shell that protects
from radiation and the hostile climate.

00:09:13.427 --> 00:09:18.015
So a lot of these designs free a cave
like condition.

00:09:18.224 --> 00:09:22.144
There are some examples of troglodyte
settlements like the Matamata underground

00:09:22.144 --> 00:09:25.606
houses and Tunisia, Africa,
which uses the Earth's mass

00:09:25.606 --> 00:09:28.150
to protect themselves from the heat
and the windstorms.

00:09:28.317 --> 00:09:32.405
But even in the settlements,
you still have access to the outdoors.

00:09:32.655 --> 00:09:36.158
This is also going to be
the very first time that humans are going

00:09:36.158 --> 00:09:39.912
to be living in complete disconnect
from the physical environment.

00:09:40.121 --> 00:09:43.082
So the way that they shell mediates
the connection to the outside

00:09:43.082 --> 00:09:46.836
environment is really important,
not just for physical safety

00:09:46.961 --> 00:09:50.339
but also for a psychological connection
to the Martian environment.

00:09:50.548 --> 00:09:54.468
The project KAHN-YATES
it's designed with multiple glazed opening

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:59.140
and the is organized over three stories
so you can get a wide

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:02.560
array of different views
into the outside environment.

00:10:02.893 --> 00:10:05.563
You also have
these more dome shaped designs,

00:10:05.563 --> 00:10:09.900
which are a little bit more common,
which has one or two fixed views.

00:10:10.151 --> 00:10:11.319
The Shell also affects

00:10:11.319 --> 00:10:14.322
the functional organization and movement
within the habitat.

00:10:14.655 --> 00:10:15.239
You'll see

00:10:15.239 --> 00:10:19.285
lots of different forms in the design,
but you can kind of divide them into two

00:10:19.327 --> 00:10:24.165
distinct categories
a vertical layout and a horizontal layout

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:28.753
Within the horizontal Layout
there's designs that separated

00:10:28.753 --> 00:10:32.256
all of the functions
into their distinct pods,

00:10:32.506 --> 00:10:37.470
and there's ones that's consolidated
all of the functions into one single pod.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:41.807
If you're looking at courtyard typologies
as a way to create social interaction,

00:10:41.807 --> 00:10:45.144
maybe an organization like this
makes a little bit more sense.

00:10:45.519 --> 00:10:50.107
These are probably the most efficient
structures in terms of applicability

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:53.819
because you can create more
and you can connect more pods.

00:10:53.986 --> 00:10:58.699
But if it were me,
my mental health would deteriorate

00:10:59.075 --> 00:11:01.786
real fast in these types of arrangements.

00:11:01.869 --> 00:11:05.039
One, because they're highly,
highly monotonous

00:11:05.456 --> 00:11:07.583
and you can see the same spaces.

00:11:07.583 --> 00:11:09.794
and same walls from all different views.

00:11:10.127 --> 00:11:13.005
Two There's very little separation
between functions.

00:11:13.756 --> 00:11:18.010
According to Edward T Hall,
we all have a spatial dimension

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:21.931
that determines our relationship
to each other and to our surroundings.

00:11:22.139 --> 00:11:27.019
When these dimensions are disrupted,
either by other people or by poor design.

00:11:27.269 --> 00:11:29.814
You can have really serious
psychological impacts.

00:11:29.814 --> 00:11:46.330
And he calls this idea proxemics

00:11:46.539 --> 00:11:48.582
In his book, The Hidden Dimension.

00:11:48.791 --> 00:11:51.544
He talks about a crowding experiment
in Norway,

00:11:51.836 --> 00:11:56.048
where they took a group of 48 rats
and put them in an indoor environment.

00:11:56.257 --> 00:12:00.344
They were given everything they needed
except for personal space.

00:12:00.594 --> 00:12:06.142
There was a quick population boom,
followed by such a severe psychological

00:12:06.142 --> 00:12:09.812
and behavioral disruption that
it eventually led to their extinction.

00:12:09.979 --> 00:12:12.440
He called these behavioral sinks.

00:12:12.606 --> 00:12:16.360
It distorted their behavior,
everything from sex nest building,

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:20.531
birth territoriality,
and a lot of the rats had tumors

00:12:20.531 --> 00:12:25.995
and enlarged kidneys and enlarged livers,
all of which are signs of extreme stress.

00:12:26.203 --> 00:12:29.457
The crowding led
to a pathological behavior in the rats.

00:12:29.623 --> 00:12:33.586
And although humans have,
for the most part, developed

00:12:33.586 --> 00:12:38.174
the level of cognitive sophistication
that makes us think before we act.

00:12:38.174 --> 00:12:41.051
The limbic system,
which is a part of the brain

00:12:41.051 --> 00:12:45.598
that controls our emotional processing
and motivation, is still one of

00:12:45.598 --> 00:12:50.144
the oldest parts of the brain, and it has
a direct connection to the brainstem.

00:12:50.311 --> 00:12:55.816
So our reactions and behaviors are still
very influenced by our instincts.

00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:59.779
And just like animals,
we still have an instinctive

00:12:59.779 --> 00:13:04.533
spatial dimension that influences
how we interact in different environments.

00:13:04.658 --> 00:13:08.287
Even if we have physical separations
or the most advanced

00:13:08.287 --> 00:13:10.331
virtual reality technology.

00:13:10.331 --> 00:13:13.751
We still need to think
about these invisible dimensions

00:13:13.751 --> 00:13:17.671
as extensions of our bodies,
and not just that

00:13:18.047 --> 00:13:22.426
these invisible dimensions are also based on things like cultural background

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:25.721
and the nature of the relationship between the people.

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:29.058
There's an example in the book Japanese walls versus

00:13:29.058 --> 00:13:32.561
German walls. In Japanese culture
for a really long time.

00:13:32.603 --> 00:13:37.691
They've used paper walls and paper doors,
so Japanese people can selectively

00:13:37.691 --> 00:13:41.529
filter out auditory information
and they speak more softly

00:13:41.946 --> 00:13:46.450
while German people
they need really thick, soundproof

00:13:46.450 --> 00:13:50.371
walls, otherwise they'd feel intruded on
in loud atmospheres.

00:13:50.538 --> 00:13:52.873
If we want to create
an enclosed environment

00:13:52.873 --> 00:13:57.253
where we try to avoid conflict
as much as possible, we really need

00:13:57.253 --> 00:14:00.673
to consider these invisible dimensions
and not just

00:14:00.881 --> 00:14:02.049
the performance.

00:14:12.935 --> 00:14:15.312
These two proposals divide the functions

00:14:15.312 --> 00:14:19.191
vertically, and these ones
divide the functions into individual pods.

00:14:19.358 --> 00:14:22.903
The horizontal layout definitely provides
more separation.

00:14:23.112 --> 00:14:26.615
In  X House, the sleeping area is the same pod

00:14:26.615 --> 00:14:30.661
as the lab, which connects the lab
and the gym in the other pod.

00:14:31.078 --> 00:14:33.539
And there's another pod for the kitchen.

00:14:33.706 --> 00:14:36.667
And the two vertical proposals
have all the functions

00:14:36.667 --> 00:14:40.254
separated into different
but interconnected floors.

00:14:40.421 --> 00:14:45.426
Comparing the two layouts, my preference
would definitely be the vertical one.

00:14:45.676 --> 00:14:50.389
And my reason is because
even in the most technical environments,

00:14:50.389 --> 00:14:53.601
in fact, especially in the most technical

00:14:53.601 --> 00:14:56.854
and rigorous environments,
I think you need a sense

00:14:56.854 --> 00:15:00.941
of variability and surprise,
something that's not planned.

00:15:01.275 --> 00:15:02.943
These people are going to be living

00:15:02.943 --> 00:15:06.906
in the same environments,
doing the same thing with the same people,

00:15:06.906 --> 00:15:10.868
eating the same friggin food
every single day for a year.

00:15:11.076 --> 00:15:12.661
Everything is scheduled,

00:15:12.661 --> 00:15:16.498
everything is planned,
and there is absolutely no surprise.

00:15:16.749 --> 00:15:17.833
This type of arrangement

00:15:17.833 --> 00:15:20.586
means that if I want to go to the kitchen,
I go to the kitchen.

00:15:21.045 --> 00:15:24.089
If I want to go to the lab,
I'll go this way and I'll go to the lab.

00:15:24.506 --> 00:15:27.092
It's really efficient
and it's really purposeful.

00:15:27.343 --> 00:15:30.763
But I think in such a high stress

00:15:30.763 --> 00:15:34.141
environment, you need a little bit of space to roam

00:15:34.141 --> 00:15:38.020
without purpose,
even if it's like 1% of the time.

00:15:38.270 --> 00:15:40.898
With this vertical layout,
if you want to get to the lab,

00:15:40.940 --> 00:15:43.609
you have to get past the garden
and the kitchen.

00:15:43.901 --> 00:15:46.737
And in those moments
you might run into another crew

00:15:46.737 --> 00:15:48.822
or you might find some other activity.

00:15:48.822 --> 00:15:49.782
That's going on.

00:15:49.782 --> 00:15:52.910
And the result is
you don't just get a variety

00:15:52.910 --> 00:15:56.622
in interactions,
but also a variety in experiences as well.

00:15:56.789 --> 00:16:01.126
You get a sense of scale and hierarchy
and a sense of surprise.

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:03.837
Instead of having everything visible
in your face.

00:16:03.879 --> 00:16:09.009
Environments like this creates a sense
of curiosity and it creates atmosphere.

00:16:09.468 --> 00:16:12.763
The Kahn Yates
project also has a vertical organization,

00:16:12.888 --> 00:16:16.809
but in this case it incorporates
a garden at the ground level

00:16:16.809 --> 00:16:19.019
that each of the floors
can look down onto.

00:16:19.228 --> 00:16:22.272
One of the competitions program
requirements was a garden,

00:16:22.481 --> 00:16:28.028
and a lot of these other projects provided
a highly efficient hydroponics garden.

00:16:28.153 --> 00:16:32.032
But again,
I really like how this design creates

00:16:32.032 --> 00:16:38.122
an informal, unplanned space,
while also dual functioning as a garden.

00:16:40.332 --> 00:16:43.043
There's concern
messaging around these explorations.

00:16:43.210 --> 00:16:46.547
We need to design for people to thrive,
not just survive.

00:16:46.588 --> 00:16:49.675
And of course, the safety and efficiency

00:16:49.675 --> 00:16:52.594
of these habitats are very,
very important.

00:16:52.761 --> 00:16:54.513
They're going to form the basis

00:16:54.513 --> 00:16:57.933
for our attitude towards
inhabiting foreign landscapes.

00:16:58.142 --> 00:17:01.854
So I think it's some of these
intangible qualities,

00:17:02.021 --> 00:17:05.441
even though they're hard to quantify,
they can really make the difference

00:17:05.441 --> 00:17:08.861
between a research mission
and a new and exciting vision

00:17:08.861 --> 00:17:11.530
for the future.

